From 6fe8f7c86b49f2b350414391cb6168fd889da3e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 12:16:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] common: new --cpu-split auto|off flag with startup throughput
 calibration

Doug's ask on top of the registry extension (previous commit): don't
just split CPU layers evenly across detected locality domains, split
them proportional to each domain's *real measured* throughput, so a
faster domain doesn't sit waiting on a slower one every token.

common_cpu_split_calibrate() enumerates CPU-type backend devices; if
fewer than 2 exist (every current fleet node except gabesrv10) it is a
correct no-op, returning an empty weight vector immediately. Otherwise,
for each CPU device: resolve its locality core mask
(ggml_backend_cpu_device_get_locality_mask()), build a throwaway
threadpool pinned to that mask (same construction pattern
common_threadpools::init already uses, just once per domain instead of
once globally, with the ggml_threadpool_new/free and
ggml_backend_cpu_set_threadpool/set_n_threads symbols resolved
dynamically via ggml_backend_reg_get_proc_address the same way that
existing code does), run a representative transformer-FFN matmul
through tools/tuning/bench.h's build_perf_cell/time_cell_median
(previously only consumed by the Metal-specific
tools/tuning/fa-vec.cpp; common/CMakeLists.txt now builds
tools/tuning/bench.cpp into libllama-common so common.cpp can reuse
it), and record 1/median_time as that domain's relative weight. This
must run before the model's real per-layer device table is built (next
commit), which is why it can't reuse the existing --warmup empty-decode
step -- that runs after the model and threadpools already exist.

New --cpu-split auto|off flag (default auto; LLAMA_ARG_CPU_SPLIT):
`off` skips calibration and forces today's single-CPU-device path
unconditionally (the regression escape hatch); `auto` runs it whenever
llama_model_params.cpu_split_weights is empty. Device count itself is
locked to whatever the registry auto-detected -- there is no
--cpu-split-count-style override to split finer than a real detected
domain, confirmed with Doug: a single sequential generation's per-token
layer dependency gives no known benefit to splitting within one shared
L3 domain.

Both the detection step (previous commit) and this calibration step now
log their real results (domain list with core ranges; per-device
measured ms/iter and computed weight) rather than only logging on
failure, closing a gap found during verification: this plan's own
Verification section requires "calibration logs a real measured ratio
per device," which was previously unobservable even on success.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet
